  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Morocco's 2022 semi-final run wasn't a fluke — it was the arrival of African football as a genuine world power. Under Walid Regragui, the Atlas Lions built one of the most organized, tactically sophisticated teams in the world. In 2026, they're not here to surprise anyone. They're here to go further.",
    "footballIdentity": "Morocco straddles two worlds — Arab and African, European-diaspora and home-grown. Many of their stars were born in France, Spain, or the Netherlands but chose to represent the kingdom. This trans-continental identity is their strength: they combine European tactical sophistication with North African grit and pride. When they beat Portugal in Qatar, the Arab world wept and celebrated together.",
    "theMoment": "Morocco is simultaneously managing the aftermath of the devastating 2023 earthquake — reconstruction is ongoing, communities are still rebuilding — while also preparing to host the 2030 World Cup. The national team is the country's most visible symbol of resilience and ambition. A deep run in 2026 would accelerate Morocco's emergence as a global football power and a statement that the earthquake didn't break them.",
    "momentType": "cultural"
  },
